  help: `<div><h3>1. Load a file</h3><p><b>Load a 3D file</b> button, or drag and drop onto the window. Formats: STEP / STP, IGES, GLB / GLTF, OBJ, STL.</p><p>The file is read by your browser and stays on your computer: nothing is sent online.</p><p><b>STEP up to ~100 MB.</b> Above that, first convert it to <b>GLB</b> (with another program or an AI), keeping the parts separate.</p></div>
<div><h3>2. Move around</h3><ul><li>Hold <b>left click</b>: orbit around the product</li><li><b>Scroll wheel</b>: zoom</li><li>Hold <b>right click</b> (or the wheel button): pan the view</li><li><b>Click a part</b>: it is located in the Parts list</li><li><b>Cube at the top right</b>: click a face, an edge or a corner to look at the product from that side. The axes follow the CAD convention (Z = vertical)</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>3. Frame the shot (Photo and Video tabs)</h3><ul><li><b>Quick views</b>: front, 3/4, side, high angle…</li><li><b>Focal length</b>: 24 mm = wide angle; 85–105 mm = telephoto</li><li><b>Image format</b>: 1:1, 4:5, 16:9… The frame on screen shows exactly what will be saved</li><li><b>Saved views</b>: keep a framing and apply it to another product for consistent series. <b>Export</b> / <b>Import</b> to share them</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>4. Position the product</h3><ul><li><b>This is the front</b>: turn the camera until you see the front of the product, upright, then click; the file will then open upright</li><li><b>Turn by 90°</b> if the product comes in lying down</li><li><b>Left drag turns</b>: the camera, or the product on one axis only; <b>Reset</b> puts it back as it was</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>5. Pick the parts</h3><p>Untick a part to hide it, <b>Solo</b> to keep only that one, <b>Show all</b> to go back. The search box filters the list by name.</p><p><b>Part opacity</b>: click a name (or the part on the model), then move the slider to make it see-through and show what is inside it. Clicks on the model go through parts below 50%. <b>Make all opaque again</b> resets every part.</p><p><b>Exploded view</b>: like a technical exploded drawing, the largest part stays and every other part leaves on the side it sticks out the most (down, up, to a side), in order, without going through the others. <b>Chosen parts</b> only moves the parts marked ✦.</p></div>
<div><h3>6. Choose the look</h3><ul><li><b>Render</b>: the shaded image, in neutral grey or with the file's colours</li><li><b>Depth</b>: depth map (white = near, black = far)</li><li><b>Lines</b>: line drawing of the outlines</li><li><b>Transparent background</b>: cut-out PNG, without the floor</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>7. Save (Photo tab)</h3><p><b>Save the image</b> downloads what is inside the frame, at the chosen size, with its <b>masks</b>, in PNG, same framing and size: <code>_mask.png</code>, the visible parts opaque on a transparent background, and <code>_mask_bw.png</code>, the same in black on white; when a part is see-through, also <code>_mask_alpha.png</code>, which follows its opacity. Everything comes in one ZIP file. Edges are smooth (in-between values following how much of the pixel is covered) and openings stay background. The camera move frames also get their masks.</p></div>
<div><h3>8. Camera moves (Video tab)</h3><p>For AI video. First straighten the product (0° = front view) and choose the format (e.g. 16:9). Pick a move: it plays at once (<b>Preview</b> to play it again). Check the keyframes with 1, 2, 3, then, under <b>Export</b>: <b>Frames</b> (the keyframes and their masks), <b>Video</b> (an MP4 reference video of the move: 1080p or 4K, 5 s per clip, 30 fps, solid background) or <b>Everything</b>. Each export comes as one ZIP file (<code>_frames.zip</code>, <code>_video.zip</code> or <code>_all.zip</code>). For <b>Push in</b> and <b>Pull back</b>, first click the part to aim at.</p><p><b>Aimed point</b>: for each move, choose the height aimed at the start and at the end (slider), or click <b>On model</b> and then a point of the product. <b>Distance</b>: the same way, the camera distance at the start and at the end (100% = whole product). The dots on the view show where each keyframe aims (green = start, red = end).</p><p><b>My moves</b>: <b>+ New move</b>, frame with the mouse and add the keyframes one by one (<b>Aim at a point</b> turns the camera towards a clicked point; the wheel or the <b>Distance</b> slider sets the distance of the chosen keyframe). <b>Copy this move</b> starts from a ready-made move. Save it: it stays in this browser, and can be exported / imported as a JSON file.</p></div>
<div><h3>8b. Keyframes (Video tab)</h3><ul><li>Each move shows its keyframes as small pictures. Click one to go there, frame the view with the mouse, then press <b>Use the current view</b>.</li><li><b>+ Add a keyframe</b> adds one after the chosen frame, as many as you like; <b>Delete the keyframe</b> removes it (at least 2 stay).</li><li>Drag a picture onto another to change the order.</li><li>After each change the clips it touches are played; each frame flashes when the camera reaches it.</li><li>A ready-made move becomes your own as soon as a frame changes; <b>Save</b> keeps it in My moves.</li><li><b>Aim at a point</b>: click a point of the product, the camera turns towards it without moving.</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>9. Product in a photo</h3><p><b>Scene</b> panel. Load a photo (or drop it on the window): it becomes the background and the camera follows the photo. Drag the <b>four corners of the square</b> onto a rectangle lying on the surface (worktop, table, tile). Corners can go outside the photo: the <b>wheel</b> zooms the view out (and in) around the pointer, <b>Space + drag</b> or the middle button moves it, <b>Fit</b> puts it back. The <b>magnifier</b> shows the photo under the corner while dragging; the sides carried on in dotted lines, the <b>horizon</b> and the grid show the plane it gives. The <b>focal length</b> is worked out when both pairs of sides converge, otherwise it is set by hand.</p><p>The product always rests on that plane: <b>drag</b> moves it, <b>right drag</b> turns it, the <b>Size</b> slider resizes it. The <b>Opacity</b> slider makes the product see-through to show what is under it: it is a preview only, the export is always at 100%. <b>Export the scene</b> downloads a ZIP at the size of the photo: the photo, the photo with the grey product (<code>_composite.jpg</code>), the grey product alone on a transparent background (<code>_grey.png</code>), its masks and a <code>_SCENE.json</code>; dropping that JSON on the window, with the same photo, brings the scene back. The other panels get the 3D view back as it was.</p></div>
<div><h3>Tip</h3><p>Give the AI the image as a position and perspective reference, and alongside it a real photo of the product for colours and materials.</p></div>`
